The big issue I have with google groups is that it removes tags from  
subject (that is text put between square brackets).
For example, I've tag this message as being out of topic for rgrn by  
adding the text "[OT]" in the subject.
This is very convenient for other people because they can see at first  
glance in the list of messages that this one does not really speak of  
Nethack and they can skip it if they want. Similarly, messages are  
sometimes tagged as being relative to Slash'em by adding the string "[S]".  
People who don't play Slash'em can skip them immediately. Most newsgroups  
I know have their own tags system.

Now, google group come and for some reason (I really can't see why, but  
they do it any way), they remove tags from subject when ansewring (iirc).  
That is, a message whose subject is "[S] AK for a monk" will suddently  
become "AK for a monk" and people seeing the answer won't think  
immediately that this is a Slash'em question and might answer in a Nethack  
context (and the AK for monk is not the same in Slash'em and Nethack).  
Thus leading to many confusion (which, in newsgroup, often end in flames).

Well, of course, for people using google group, the original subject will  
show at start of the thread (I guess), so that there won't be any problem,  
the tag will still be here. But for people not using google groups, the  
threading system might be different and the disappearance of the tag can  
be harmful.
Even more, some people have set up automatic filtering of messages (most  
newsreader allow this) and can delete automatically some messages, eg  
those speaking of Slash'em because they don't play Slash'em. Now, the  
answer will still be here for them and that will be all the more confusing.

The worst is that other kind of brackets are not removed by google groups.  
Curly brackets, for example, are kept. And people have adviced and used  
them for tagging since google group exist (that is, write "{S} AK for  
monk").
Now, either square brackets are indeed harmfull for some reason (still  
unknown to me, if someone knows it, please explain) and google is  
perfectly right to remove them from subject,
or square brackets are perfectly safe and I really really can't see why on  
earth google decided to remove them, thus forcing us to change a 20+ years  
old habit of writing messages.

